    "created_at": "2021-01-19T12:14:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-01-19T12:14:52Z",
    "body": "This makes sense to me. Such behavior might be seen as more predictable (in addition to the implementation benefits). In a way this is similar to a clarification that was added to the CSS multicol spec a few years ago.\r\n\r\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/css-multicol-1/#spanning-columns : \r\n> Note: Whether the element establishes a new formatting context does not depend on whether the element is a descendent of a multicol or not. When column-span is all, it always does. This helps with robustness of designs to later revisions that remove the multicol, or when media queries turn the multicol off in some situations.\r\n\r\nPrior to this, some (or all?) implementation would only let column-span:all force a new formatting context if the element would actually become a column spanner (i.e. only if it was an in-flow descendant in a formatting context established by a multicol container). This led to implementation complexity without doing authors any favors.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
